# SOLN Website Coding Directives

## Project authority
- State of Loc Nation Global Public Benefit Corporation (SOLNGPBC) owns and controls this repository.
- The repository owner has final approval over all content, design, releases, and merges.
- Coders implement approved materials; they must not independently create policy, laws, financial terms, or official positions.
- GitHub publication or technical deployment as SOLNGPBC governmental, judicial, financial, or regulatory recognition.

## Authoritative source files
- Use only SOLN-approved documents and content supplied by the repository owner. (wwww.stateoflocnation.com)
- Do not rewrite, summarize, reinterpret, or correct governing instruments without written approval.
- Preserve each official document’s title, date, version, identifier, and original file.
- If two documents conflict, flag the conflict for review; do not choose one silently.
- Clearly distinguish adopted instruments, proposals, research, court filings, press releases, and archived material.

## Required structure
- `/index.html` — public homepage
- `/assets/` — styles, scripts, images, icons, and fonts
- `/founding-documents/` — approved founding records
- `/laws/` — enacted SOLN instruments
- `/departments/` — departmental information
- `/court-records/` — public court materials
- `/currency/` — approved LND information
- `/media/` — viral press releases, videos, and public media
- `/archive/` — superseded materials retained for history

## Document integrity
- Never replace an original document silently.
- Preserve prior versions in the archive.
- Display an effective date, publication date, version number, and status where applicable.
- Generate a SHA-256 checksum for every official downloadable document.
- Record document additions and changes in `CHANGELOG.md`.
- Do not modify court-filed documents. Publish exact copies and label their filing status accurately.

## Content and claims
- Do not invent facts, citations, approvals, partnerships, licenses, regulatory status, or financial balances, preserve document information.
- Keep SOLNGPBC declarations clearly attributed to SOLNGPBC.
- Add source citations and dates to factual claims.
- Flag uncertain claims with `REVIEW REQUIRED`; 
## LND and financial information
- Do not create, issue, transfer, settle, redeem, or change LND balances through the public website.
- Do not alter valuation, parity, interest, issuance, redemption, or eligibility rules without written authorization.
- Clearly separate informational pages from operational ledger systems.
- Never represent a pending transaction as final, settled, or irrevocable.
- Any payment, wallet, KYC, tax, or ledger integration requires separate security and compliance review.
- Never expose account numbers, tax identifiers, KYC records, private balances, API keys, credentials, or ledger administration links.

## Privacy and security
- Remove private addresses, personal phone numbers, signatures, birth dates, identification numbers, banking information, and protected information before publication.
- Store no secrets in source code or Git history.
- Use GitHub Secrets for authorized deployment credentials.
- Do not add tracking, cookies, analytics, external scripts, or data-collection forms without approval.
- All forms must link to approved Terms, Privacy, and Consent notices.
- Report suspected vulnerabilities privately; never publish exploit details or sensitive data.

## Development standards
- Use semantic HTML, organized CSS, and minimal JavaScript.
- Use relative asset paths compatible with the GitHub Pages project URL.
- Do not hard-code localhost addresses, temporary links, or private URLs.
- Avoid unnecessary frameworks and dependencies.
- Pin dependency versions and document why each dependency is required.
- Verify all navigation, downloads, images, videos, and external links.
- The site must work on mobile phones, tablets, and desktop browsers.
- Meet WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility standards where practicable.
- Provide alt text, keyboard navigation, visible focus states, sufficient contrast, and clear headings.
- Do not use copyrighted images, video, fonts, or text without permission or a valid license.

## Git workflow
- Work on a separate branch.
- Submit changes through a pull request.
- Do not commit directly to `main` unless the repository owner expressly authorizes it.
- Use clear commit messages describing the actual change.
- Do not force-push, delete branches, rewrite history, or remove files without approval.
- Keep unrelated changes out of the same pull request.
- Require owner review before merging content involving governance, law, finance, privacy, or security.

## Testing and deployment
- Confirm that the root `index.html` is the intended homepage.
- Test the exact GitHub Pages project URL, not only a local preview.
- Run link, HTML, accessibility, and security checks before merging.
- GitHub Actions must fail when required checks fail.
- Verify the deployment after every merge.
- Maintain a documented rollback method and preserve the last working release.
- A green workflow check confirms technical deployment only; it does not validate the accuracy or legal effect of the content.

## Prohibited actions
- Do not delete historical records.
- Do not change official seals, names, identifiers, signatures, or document language.
- Do not create hidden administrator access or undocumented code.
- Do not connect production financial, identity, email, or government systems without explicit authorization.
- Do not upload malware, unlicensed materials, fabricated records, or confidential information.
- Do not merge your own pull request unless specifically authorized.

## Required handoff
Every pull request must state:
1. What changed.
2. Which source documents were used.
3. What was not changed.
4. Which tests were completed.
5. Any broken links, uncertainties, risks, or decisions requiring owner review.
6. Screenshots of the desktop and mobile result.
